## 2. Bug 2 — unadjusted stock splits · FIXED

**Symptom.** 148 splits across 140 symbols were unadjusted. KOTAKBANK showed a pre-split close of
Rs 10,806 beside a post-split Rs 421.

**Root cause.** The adjustment relied on NSE publishing an ADJUSTED `PREV_CLOSE` on the ex-date.
It does not always do so. On KOTAKBANK's 1:5 split (14 Jan 2026) NSE printed the raw Rs 2,132.60,
so the detection ratio read 1.00 and nothing fired.

**Fix.** A second detector on the close series: a single-session fall past 35% whose inverse ratio
lands near a whole number >= 2 is a split, not a price move.

**One iteration was needed and is worth recording.** The first attempt used a 6% tolerance and MISSED
KOTAKBANK, because the observed ratio was **5.066, not 5.000** — the stock also moved that day. The
detector now **snaps to the nearest integer** and uses that as the factor, so the residual price move
survives adjustment. Tolerance widened to 15%; the discriminator is the SIZE of the move, not the
tolerance, because NSE price bands make a real 35%+ single-session fall a corporate action.

**Result.**

| | Before | After |
|---|---|---|
| Corporate action events | 354 | **548** (194 via the close series) |
| Unadjusted splits market-wide | **148 / 140 symbols** | **0 / 0** |
| KOTAKBANK worst session ratio | **0.197** | **0.987** |

**A correction to an earlier claim.** On 8 Sep SHILPAMED was reported at +192% over six months, with
"roughly half" attributed to the split. The true adjusted figure is **+224.5%** — the old panel had
the adjustment INVERTED rather than absent, so the correction was in the wrong direction.

**One residual, knowingly accepted.** NAZARA still shows -40.5% in a single session at a ratio of
1.68, which is not near a whole number. Either a non-integer corporate action (demerger, 3:2 bonus)
or a genuine fall. Needs a manual check before anything is computed on it.

