## 3. BROAD tier and the delivery-adjusted liquidity floor · NEW RULE

### The problem

`caps.csv` was built only from the Nifty 100 / Midcap 150 / Smallcap 250 lists. Every symbol outside
the Nifty 500 arrived downstream with no tier and was silently defaulted to SMALL, which the RISK-OFF
mandate ("large/mid only") then blocked.

**That is 42% of the tradeable universe and 95% of the top 40 three-month movers** — TBZ,
KABRAEXTRU, CYIENTDLM, INDSWFTLAB, BODALCHEM, RAYMOND and the rest — excluded for being un-indexed
rather than for being small.

### What was rejected

**A turnover-based size proxy.** Tested against the known Nifty 500 tiers it reached only **62%
accuracy overall and 41% on MID**. Too weak to present as a size judgement, so it was not used.

### What was adopted

`caps.csv` now carries every panel symbol, with:

| column | meaning |
|---|---|
| `cap_tier` | LARGE / MID / SMALL / **BROAD** |
| `cap_source` | `nse_index` or `not_in_nse_index` |
| `adv_cr` | 45-day average turnover, Rs cr |
| `dlv_adv_cr` | **the same multiplied by delivery%** — the part that actually settles |

**BROAD is a membership fact, not a size claim.** Eligibility is then decided on liquidity, which is
measurable, rather than on an invented size label.

**Rule: a BROAD name is eligible when `dlv_adv_cr >= 10`.**

### Why delivery-adjusted rather than raw ADV

Two names can show the same ADV and differ tenfold in real liquidity:

| | ADV | Delivery | Real liquidity |
|---|---|---|---|
| ITDC | Rs 37cr | 25.5% | **Rs 3.4cr** |
| SJS | Rs 42cr | 55.1% | **Rs 22.7cr** |

### Why Rs 10cr and not Rs 6cr

The first proposal was Rs 6cr, derived from exit arithmetic — "can a full 15% position be sold over
two days". **That was the wrong question.** At this book size liquidity was never binding: even
Rs 5cr ADV clears a full position inside a day.

The threshold was then set from outcomes instead. Across **33 Fridays and 11,646 observations** of
BROAD names above a rising 50 DMA, 4-week forward excess return by band:

| dlv_adv_cr | n | 4wk fwd | median | hit |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1-3 | 4,185 | +2.05% | +0.14% | 51% |
| 3-6 | 3,156 | +1.78% | -0.04% | 50% |
| **6-10** | 1,811 | **+1.43%** | **-0.34%** | **48%** |
| 10-20 | 1,629 | +3.04% | +0.88% | 53% |
| 20-40 | 645 | +3.57% | +1.83% | 56% |
| 40+ | 220 | **+8.77%** | +3.41% | **61%** |

**Above Rs 6cr beats below by +1.53pp at t = +2.89.** But the Rs 6-10 band is the worst of the six,
so a Rs 6cr floor lands in the weakest bucket. Moving to Rs 10cr lifts the kept-set forward return
from **+2.74% to +3.68%**. Rs 20cr scores better still but retains only ~7% of the broad universe,
which is too narrow to trade.

**Interpretation.** Real liquidity is behaving as a QUALITY signal, not an exit constraint. Names
with genuinely settled volume are names institutions are accumulating.

### Measured effect

Two-week daily backtest, top-10 picks per day ranked by 3-month relative strength:

| | mean 1-day forward |
|---|---|
| OLD rule (large/mid only) | **-0.547%** |
| NEW rule | **+0.821%** |
| difference | **+1.369pp/day**, better on **9 of 10 days** |

Eligible universe: **240 -> 557**. On last week's scan, **all 40 of the new top 40 and 97 of the top
100 are BROAD names.** Twelve sit at a clean entry: MOREPENLAB, CYIENTDLM, SHILPAMED, BIRLACABLE,
DIACABS, YASHO, SBCL, CUPID, IOLCP, RPEL, MARINE, AVALON. At the Rs 10cr floor, RPEL (7.8) and YASHO
(9.5) drop out.

### To review

- **The floor is tied to book size.** At Rs 50 lakh, Rs 10cr is right. Past roughly Rs 1 crore of
  book it should be re-derived.
- **33 Fridays is one market episode.** Five of eight parameter changes tested on this project have
  reversed when the window widened. Re-test on the full 1,414-session panel at the next review.
- **Downstream skills still expect LARGE/MID/SMALL.** The position sizer and regime monitor need to
  handle a fourth value: map BROAD to the SMALL risk budget, and gate regime eligibility on
  `dlv_adv_cr >= 10` rather than on cap tier.
